{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1594776539/14/codegen_prompts/8.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/14`\n- 题号：`8`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1594776539/14/8.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"8\">\n- `description`：网格中的彩色小方块携带\"传播指令\"，每个方块向其颜色编码的方向发射射线，射线遇到另一个方块时改变该方块的颜色，最终根据所有传播完成后的方块颜色状态生成输出。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为黑色。\n  - 网格中散布着若干个 1×1 的彩色像素（非黑色），每个像素的颜色编码了一个传播方向：Red = 向右，Blue = 向上，Green = 向下，Yellow = 向左。\n  - 像素之间至少间隔 2 格，保证射线路径上不会有歧义。\n  - 一个特殊的 Magenta 像素作为\"种子\"，它不发射射线，而是接收射线并记录最终状态。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 对每个非 Magenta 的彩色像素，按其颜色编码的方向发射一条射线（单像素宽），射线用 Grey 色绘制，穿过黑色背景格子直到碰到网格边界或另一个彩色像素为止。\n  - 如果射线碰到另一个彩色像素（包括 Magenta），将被碰到的像素颜色改为射线发射者的颜色。\n  - 射线发射按从上到下、从左到右的扫描顺序依次处理每个像素（使用该像素当前的颜色确定方向，而非初始颜色）。\n  - 所有射线处理完毕后，输出最终的网格状态。\n\n- `Key insight`：每个像素的颜色既是\"身份\"又是\"行为指令\"，被射线命中后颜色改变意味着行为也随之改变——这形成了一个级联效应。解题者需要理解颜色-方向映射关系，然后按正确的处理顺序模拟传播过程，注意先处理的射线会改变后续像素的行为方向。这是\"颜色-方向解码→顺序敏感的级联传播→状态追踪\"的多层推理。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/825aa9e9, eval/136b0064, eval/3e6067c3, train/b25e450b, train/d968ffd4, train/7e576d6e, train/ac6f9922, eval/da515329\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"825aa9e9\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3965\nmechanism=0.4474\nlexical=0.0116\ncode_feature=0.7500\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:ray_loop, object:grid_edge, object:path, operation:draw_line, operation:ray_cast, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/825aa9e9.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/825aa9e9.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nDrop the moving-color objects downward until they rest above a one-row cushion before the obstacle or bottom edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has exactly three colors: empty background, a stationary obstacle/floor color, and a moving object color.\n- The obstacle color is the unique non-background role that touches the bottom edge.\n- Moving pixels start outside the obstacle cells, outside the row directly above each obstacle pixel, and outside the bottom row.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background and obstacle pixels fixed.\n- Treat obstacle pixels, cells directly above obstacle pixels, and the bottom row as blocked.\n- Repeatedly move every 4-connected moving-color object one row down when its translated shape does not hit blocked cells or another moving object.\n\nKey insight: The visible floor has an invisible one-row cushion above it, so the objects fall under gravity but stop with one empty row before the floor or the grid bottom.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected objects in the moving color\",\n  \"path\": \"vertical downward path through background cells until the next step would hit blocked space or another moving object\",\n  \"dst\": \"settle above the one-row cushion before obstacle pixels or the bottom edge\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"move all currently movable objects one row at a time simultaneously; same-color objects may stack or merge\",\n  \"transform\": \"keep background and obstacle pixels fixed; obstacle pixels, cells directly above obstacle pixels, and the bottom row are blocked\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"136b0064\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3900\nmechanism=0.4642\nlexical=0.0119\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:ray_loop, object:marker, operation:draw_line, operation:ray_cast, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/136b0064.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/136b0064.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nDraw a polyline by decoding 3x3 glyph instructions read across a divided grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- Left of a full-height vertical divider: 3x3 colored glyphs on bg.\n- Right of divider: a single gray (5) start pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the divider; shift the gray start into the left canvas.\n- Read glyphs in column-major order (left→right, top→bottom).\n- Each glyph's shape (pixel area) encodes a segment: area 4 → down 2, area 5 → right 3, area 6 → left 4, area 7 → left 2.\n- Append segments sequentially, stepping down one cell between each.\n\nKey insight: Glyph pixel count (area) uniquely determines both segment length and direction — the shape itself is the instruction.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"3x3 glyphs left of a vertical divider, plus a gray start marker on the right\",\n  \"dst\": \"a single polyline in the left canvas starting at the shifted gray marker\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"glyphs ordered by tile column (left→right), then tile row (top→bottom)\",\n  \"transform\": \"remove divider, shift gray into the left canvas, then append one segment per glyph while moving downward; segment length+orientation are determined by glyph type (area 4→vertical len2; area 5→horizontal len3 right; area 6→horizontal len4 left; area 7→horizontal len2 left)\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3e6067c3\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3783\nmechanism=0.4646\nlexical=0.0118\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:ray_loop, object:path, object:square, operation:draw_line, operation:ray_cast, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/3e6067c3.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/3e6067c3.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConnect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n\nRule:\n- Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n\nKey insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"adjacent pixel pair s in a sequence of control pixels in bottom area\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"keep src\",\n  \"dst\": \"merge with adjacent square pair d in an array of squares with colored inner square in main area\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.colors == d.inner_squares.colors\",\n  \"transform\": \"connect d.inner_squares with s[0].color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"process control pairs from left to right\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"control pixels indicates connection path\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b25e450b\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3729\nmechanism=0.3678\nlexical=0.0140\ncode_feature=1.0000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:ray_loop, object:grid_edge, object:path, operation:draw_line, operation:sort_order, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/b25e450b.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/b25e450b.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide each black border rectangle to the opposite border, recoloring its swept stripe orange and leaving the rectangle black at the far edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains black rectangular 4-connected masks, each touching exactly one outer border.\n- Grey cells form incidental pattern content; orange is the trail color used when a black mask slides.\n\nRule\n- Treat all black rectangles as moving simultaneously.\n- A rectangle touching the top or bottom edge moves vertically to the opposite edge in the same columns.\n- A rectangle touching the left or right edge moves horizontally to the opposite edge in the same rows.\n- Recolor the complete swept row or column stripe to orange, then stamp every destination rectangle in black.\n\nKey insight\n- Edge contact gives the slide direction; the output is the set of opposite-edge black rectangles plus their orange trails.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"black rectangular mask s touching exactly one outer border\",\n  \"path\": \"straight slide from s to the opposite border along its row band or column band, determined by the touched border\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"recolor the complete swept row/column stripe orange, then place s at the opposite edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"all black masks move simultaneously; destination black masks overwrite orange trails and any grey cells, while cells outside swept stripes remain unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d968ffd4\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3824\nmechanism=0.4266\nlexical=0.0115\ncode_feature=0.7500\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:ray_loop, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:path, operation:draw_line, operation:ray_cast, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/d968ffd4.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/d968ffd4.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtend each of two aligned rectangular marker objects toward the other, filling half of the gap across the full perpendicular span.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background contains exactly two solid, same-sized monochromatic rectangles.\n- The rectangles share either the same row span or the same column span, with a background gap between their facing edges.\n\nRule\n- If the rectangles are side-by-side, color the left half of the inter-object gap across every row with the left object's color, and the right half with the right object's color.\n- If one rectangle is above the other, color the upper half of the gap across every column with the upper object's color, and the lower half with the lower object's color.\n- When the gap length is odd, leave the center row/column as background.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker objects are endpoints of an axial corridor; their own size identifies the facing edge, while the filled corridor spans the whole grid perpendicular to that axis.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
